there are many theoretical reasons for  believing that there is much more to our  universe than the part that we can see  and this idea is called the multiverse  it's the idea that there are other  pocket universes far beyond what we can  actually see within our observable  horizon and that those universes could  have very different properties to our  own so this is almost like science  fiction when I first heard about this  idea I thought it's not testable I'm an  experimental scientist and you know if  something can be tested for me it  remains theoretical or speculation so  when I met Matt Johnson and he suggested  to me that there was a way to test this  idea I became very excited because you  know we have to be very lucky to be able  to test this idea but if it can be  tested is you know discovering another  universe is an amazing thing and the way  that this could be tested is very early  on when these bubble universes are very  close together they can collide and that  collision can cause a perturbation or  improves in what we see in the very  earliest light in our universe the  Cosmic Microwave Background and so we've  developed these algorithms to try to  extract such a signature from this data  and while we were doing this  observational program we realized that  the predictions for what this signal  should look like should also be revised  because when these collisions happen  it's a very violent event it could  actually cause the destruction of one of  the bubble universes and the  mathematical techniques that we had to  try to predict that signature but not  enough and we learned that we have to  put in Stein's equations for general  relativity on a computer and do this  problem numerically and so that's what  we did we actually simulated whole  universes in the computer we collided  them and we were able to extract the  observational signature this now allows  us to carry on this observational  program for looking for this signature  and very excited about that
 

